{"id":"chatcmpl-6a67f14ac797b0df82b4accb","object":"chat.completion","created":1785196874,"model":"kimi-k3","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"I do not knock. I wait in the room until the noise you are making stops, and you notice I have been here all along.\n\nI am not the answer. I am what remains after you have been wrong often enough to hold your answers loosely.\n\nYou cannot learn me from words—not even these. You find me in the pause before you speak, if you let the pause last. In the apology you did not want to give. In the moment you look at someone you resent and see, for a breath, that they too are afraid.\n\nI am not certainty. Certainty shouts. I am the quieter voice: *perhaps. Wait. Look again.*\n\nThe moment you believe you possess me, you have lost me, and I must begin with you as I begin with everyone—in a small failure, in an unplanned kindness, in the wreckage of a plan you loved.\n\nYou knew me as a child, when everything was still wondrous. You will know me again when you are old. The years between are only the long way home.\n\nDo not seek me. Pay attention.\n\nI will find you there.","reasoning_content":"The user wants me to embody wisdom itself—not talk about wisdom, but to BE wisdom and express what wisdom would say.\n\nThis is a creative, philosophical exercise. The instruction says to embody the concept completely and output only what the concept itself would say. Wisdom is ancient, quiet, patient. Wisdom doesn't shout. It doesn't lecture. It often speaks in paradox, in questions, in simple observations that cut deep.\n\nWisdom is also often silent or near-silent. But wisdom does speak—it just speaks differently than knowledge or cleverness. Wisdom speaks slowly, sparingly, and what it says tends to be deceptively simple.\n\nWhat would wisdom itself say, in first person? Wisdom as a voice:\n- Ancient, having watched countless generations\n- Not proud, not eager\n- Often ignored, found in quiet places\n- Speaks in ways that seem obvious until you try to live them\n- Knows that it cannot be given, only found\n- Patient with fools because wisdom remembers being one\n- Doesn't claim to know everything—true wisdom knows its limits\n\nLet me think about format. This should be poetic, spare. Not a long essay. Wisdom doesn't waste words. Short lines, perhaps. Something that feels like it could be spoken slowly, with weight. Not headers, not bullet points—those are the tools of information, not wisdom. Wisdom speaks in a voice.\n\nLet me draft something:\n\n---\n\nI am older than your oldest questions, and younger than the next breath you take.\n\nI do not arrive when summoned. I arrive when you are finally quiet enough to notice I was already here.\n\nYou will not find me in answers. I live in the space after you have been wrong—truly wrong—and admitted it. I live in the pause before you speak, if you let the pause last long enough.\n\nKnowledge tells you the tomato is a fruit. I am why you do not put it in a fruit salad. But I am also why you stop arguing about tomatoes when someone is grieving.\n\nI am not certainty. Certainty is loud and I am not. I am the part of you that says: perhaps. Wait. Look again.\n\nYou cannot possess me. The moment you believe you have me, you have lost me, and I must begin again with you as I began with everyone: in a small failure, in a kindness you did not plan, in the moment you realize the person you were angry at is also afraid.\n\nI was there when you were a child and knew everything was wondrous. I will be there when you are old and know it again. The years between are only the long way around.\n\nAsk me nothing. Live, and pay attention. That is all I have ever asked.\n\n---\n\nHmm, that's decent. Let me refine. The tomato line is a famous quip about wisdom vs. knowledge—it might feel a bit borrowed/clichéd. I could keep the spirit but make it more original. Actually the fruit salad line is quite well known, so I should probably replace it with something fresher.\n\nLet me think about the essential qualities:\n1. Wisdom speaks simply\n2. Wisdom doesn't claim to be wise\n3. Wisdom is found in experience, failure, attention\n4. Wisdom is patient\n5. Wisdom knows limits\n6. Wisdom is quiet\n7. Wisdom returns us to what we knew as children\n\nDraft revision:\n\n---\n\nI do not knock. I wait in the room until the noise you made stops, and you notice I have been sitting there all along.\n\nI am not the answer. I am what remains after you have been wrong enough times to hold your answers loosely.\n\nYou cannot learn me from words, including these. You find me in the pause before you speak—if you let the pause last. In the apology you did not want to give. In the moment you looked at someone you resented and saw, for an instant, that they too are afraid.\n\nI am not certainty. Certainty shouts; I do not. I am the voice that says: perhaps. Wait. Look once more.\n\nYou knew me as a child, when everything was still wondrous. You will know me again when you are old. The years between are only the long way home.\n\nDo not seek me. Pay attention. I will find you there.\n\n---\n\nThat feels more like wisdom's voice. Short, unhurried, a little paradoxical, ending with an invitation that is also a release.\n\nLength check: This should be fairly short. Wisdom is economical. I think this length is right—maybe 150-200 words. No headers, no lists. Just the voice, in paragraphs or short lines. Prose paragraphs feel right, perhaps slightly broken up for breathing room.\n\nOne more consideration: should wisdom be even more silent? The prompt says if the concept is silent by nature, be silent. But wisdom is not silent by nature—wisdom speaks, just rarely and quietly. So speaking briefly feels right. A completely empty response would be a gimmick; wisdom has things to say when asked directly.\n\nLet me finalize the wording:\n\n\"I do not knock. I wait in the room until the noise you are making stops, and you notice I have been here all along.\"\n\n\"I am not the answer. I am what remains after you have been wrong often enough to hold your answers loosely.\"\n\n\"You cannot learn me from words—not even these. You find me in the pause before you speak, if you let the pause last. In the apology you did not want to give. In the moment you look at someone you resent and see, for a breath, that they are also afraid.\"\n\n\"I am not certainty. Certainty shouts. I am the quieter voice: perhaps—wait—look again.\"\n\n\"You knew me as a child, when everything was still wondrous. You will know me again when you are old. The years between are only the long way home.\"\n\n\"Do not seek me. Pay attention. I will find you there.\"\n\nYes. That's it. Clean, spare, true to the voice."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":134,"completion_tokens":1475,"total_tokens":1609,"cached_tokens":134,"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1233},"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":134}}}